course: dictionary
lesson: 15511
title: Subordinating conjunctions
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A subordinating conjunction is a joining word that links a small part of a sentence to a bigger part. Words like because, when, if, although, while, since, before and until are all examples.
explain: «I stayed inside because it was raining.» The part «because it was raining» cannot stand alone as a sentence. It needs the main part, «I stayed inside», to make sense.
explain: A memory trick is I SAW A WABUB: if, since, as, when, although, while, after, before, until, because. Coordinating conjunctions such as and, but and or join equal parts instead, like two sweets in one bag.
fact: A subordinating conjunction starts a subordinate clause.
fact: A subordinate clause is a part of a sentence that cannot stand alone.
fact: «Because» and «when» are common subordinating conjunctions.
fact: «Subordinate» can also mean lower in rank or importance.
fact: Formal meaning: subordinating something means treating it as less important than something else.
exercise: «We will play outside when the rain stops.» Which word is the subordinating conjunction? | play | *when | outside | rain
exercise: Which of these is NOT a subordinating conjunction? | *and | until | because | since
exercise: «The manager subordinated the small jobs to the big project.» What does subordinated mean? | Made them much bigger | Forgot them completely | Finished them early | *Treated them as less important
test: «I will call you after I finish my tea.» Which word starts the subordinate clause? | tea | I | call | *after
test: Which joining word completes this sentence best? «The dog barked ___ the postman came.» | and | but | *when | or
exam: Which sentence uses a subordinating conjunction? | I like cake and I like tea. | *Sam cried because he lost his toy. | Mum and Dad went out.
